# Check out LF everywhere, including on Windows.
#
# Without this, Git's Windows default rewrites files to CRLF on checkout, and
# `gofmt -l` then reports every file in the repository as unformatted — which
# is what the first Windows CI run did. A contributor on Windows would see the
# same thing, and their first `make fmt` would rewrite the entire tree.
#
# LF is also what this project's own output uses: the generated alias files are
# LF on every platform, deliberately, so that rendering is byte-deterministic
# regardless of the machine that produced it. Preserving a user's CRLF happens
# in their profile, which AliasDeck does not own — not in files it writes.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Golden files are byte-exact expectations. Any normalisation at all would make
# them compare unequal to what the renderer produced.
*.golden -text
